1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,350
Welcome to lectures twenty seven and twenty eight:

2
00:00:05,350 --> 00:00:08,025
dimensionality reduction.

3
00:00:08,025 --> 00:00:12,840
P C A, L D A, t-S N E and U M A P, and V I F.

4
00:00:13,640 --> 00:00:18,068
Lecture twenty seven is about fewer, cleaner features:

5
00:00:18,068 --> 00:00:25,940
multicollinearity and V I F, the idea of P C A, variance explained, and interpreting components.

6
00:00:25,940 --> 00:00:30,040
Lecture twenty eight is about labels and pictures:

7
00:00:30,040 --> 00:00:37,666
L D A, the classification view, t-S N E and U M A P for pictures only, and which method when.

8
00:00:37,666 --> 00:00:39,716
Our data is Wine Quality:

9
00:00:39,716 --> 00:00:47,096
six thousand four hundred ninety seven wines, red and white, with eleven lab measurements.

10
00:00:47,896 --> 00:00:53,571
Multicollinearity is when one feature can be predicted from the others.

11
00:00:53,571 --> 00:01:01,564
The density of wine depends on its sugar and its alcohol, so density adds almost no new information.

12
00:01:01,564 --> 00:01:07,478
In regression, linked features make the weights unstable and hard to read.

13
00:01:07,478 --> 00:01:14,752
A correlation heat map only checks pairs; V I F checks each feature against all the others.

14
00:01:14,752 --> 00:01:23,464
V I F equals one over one minus R squared, where R squared says how well the other features predict this one.

15
00:01:24,264 --> 00:01:37,107
On the wine data, density has a V I F of fifteen point nine, residual sugar seven point two, fixed acidity four point nine, and alcohol four point eight.

16
00:01:37,107 --> 00:01:37,946
The guide:

17
00:01:37,946 --> 00:01:47,767
one means no link, one to five is moderate and usually fine, five to ten is high, check it, and above ten is serious.

18
00:01:47,767 --> 00:01:52,804
Drop density and the largest V I F falls to two point eight.

19
00:01:52,804 --> 00:01:58,008
Or use P C A, whose components always have V I F equal to one.

20
00:01:58,808 --> 00:02:03,899
Principal Component Analysis rotates the data to new axes.

21
00:02:03,899 --> 00:02:09,165
P C one is the direction in which the data spreads the most.

22
00:02:09,165 --> 00:02:14,870
P C two is the next biggest spread, at ninety degrees to P C one.

23
00:02:14,870 --> 00:02:20,663
Keep a few, since the first few P Cs hold most of the information.

24
00:02:20,663 --> 00:02:29,528
Each P C is a weighted mix of all features, P Cs are uncorrelated, and P C A does not use the target.

25
00:02:30,328 --> 00:02:32,478
Always scale before P C A.

26
00:02:32,478 --> 00:02:35,289
P C A chases the biggest variance.

27
00:02:35,289 --> 00:02:46,533
Unscaled, total sulfur dioxide, with a standard deviation of fifty seven, beats density, with a standard deviation of point oh oh three.

28
00:02:46,533 --> 00:02:51,412
P C one explains ninety five percent just because of units.

29
00:02:51,412 --> 00:03:01,168
After standard scaling, every column has standard deviation one, and P C one explains twenty seven point five percent.

30
00:03:01,968 --> 00:03:03,125
How many P Cs?

31
00:03:03,125 --> 00:03:09,735
The scree plot shows bars of percent explained and a line of cumulative percent.

32
00:03:09,735 --> 00:03:10,726
Three rules:

33
00:03:10,726 --> 00:03:22,955
the elbow of the scree plot suggests three; the Kaiser rule, eigenvalue above one, gives three; and reaching ninety percent of variance needs seven.

34
00:03:22,955 --> 00:03:29,400
P C one plus P C two hold fifty percent of the information in eleven features.

35
00:03:30,200 --> 00:03:33,812
To interpret components, read the loadings.

36
00:03:33,812 --> 00:03:42,380
A loading is the weight of a feature in a P C, and you read the largest positive and negative weights.

37
00:03:42,380 --> 00:03:50,528
P C one has sulfur dioxide and sugar on one side and volatile acidity and chlorides on the other:

38
00:03:50,528 --> 00:03:52,964
white style versus red style.

39
00:03:52,964 --> 00:03:55,988
P C two has density against alcohol:

40
00:03:55,988 --> 00:03:57,584
heavy versus light.

41
00:03:57,584 --> 00:04:01,784
P C three has citric and fixed acidity against pH:

42
00:04:01,784 --> 00:04:02,456
acidity.

43
00:04:03,256 --> 00:04:04,522
Check the name:

44
00:04:04,522 --> 00:04:07,053
P C A never saw the wine type.

45
00:04:07,053 --> 00:04:18,190
Yet red wines sit on the left, with an average P C one of minus two point five, and white wines on the right, with plus point eight.

46
00:04:18,190 --> 00:04:22,240
P C one really is a red versus white style axis.

47
00:04:23,040 --> 00:04:25,739
Reading a biplot has three rules.

48
00:04:25,739 --> 00:04:33,181
Arrows in the same direction mean the features rise together, like the two sulfur dioxides.

49
00:04:33,181 --> 00:04:39,969
Opposite directions mean one rises while the other falls, like density and alcohol.

50
00:04:39,969 --> 00:04:44,304
And points lie towards the features they are high in.

51
00:04:45,104 --> 00:04:46,834
Lecture twenty eight:

52
00:04:46,834 --> 00:04:52,352
using labels to separate classes, and drawing pictures of clusters.

53
00:04:53,152 --> 00:04:57,122
L D A reduces dimensions to separate classes.

54
00:04:57,122 --> 00:05:09,120
P C A does not use class labels and finds directions of largest spread; L D A uses labels and finds directions of best class separation.

55
00:05:09,120 --> 00:05:14,589
L D A's maximum components is the number of classes minus one.

56
00:05:14,589 --> 00:05:19,618
P C A is used to compress and explain; L D A to classify.

57
00:05:19,618 --> 00:05:28,528
For example, quality grouped as low, medium and high is three classes, so L D A gives two components.

58
00:05:29,328 --> 00:05:32,174
L D A beats P C A at classification.

59
00:05:32,174 --> 00:05:50,592
Using the same two dimensions to predict quality group, two L D A components give fifty seven point seven percent test accuracy, two P C A components give forty eight point six, and always guessing medium gives forty three point six.

60
00:05:50,592 --> 00:05:54,624
L D A chose its dimensions to separate the classes.

61
00:05:55,424 --> 00:05:58,750
t-S N E and U M A P are for pictures only.

62
00:05:58,750 --> 00:06:05,876
They keep neighbours close, giving clear clusters, as in two thousand red and white wines.

63
00:06:05,876 --> 00:06:17,912
But the axes have no meaning, gaps and sizes are not reliable, the picture changes with the random seed and settings, and t-S N E cannot place new rows.

64
00:06:18,712 --> 00:06:20,623
Which method when?

65
00:06:20,623 --> 00:06:24,021
To find and fix linked features:

66
00:06:24,021 --> 00:06:27,313
V I F, then drop, or use P C A.

67
00:06:27,313 --> 00:06:31,773
To compress features and keep the meaning:

68
00:06:31,773 --> 00:06:33,897
P C A with loadings.

69
00:06:33,897 --> 00:06:37,932
To separate known classes or classify:

70
00:06:37,932 --> 00:06:38,569
L D A.

71
00:06:38,569 --> 00:06:42,074
To look at clusters in a picture:

72
00:06:42,074 --> 00:06:44,091
t-S N E or U M A P.

73
00:06:44,091 --> 00:06:45,790
To feed a model:

74
00:06:45,790 --> 00:06:48,976
P C A or L D A, never t-S N E.

75
00:06:49,776 --> 00:06:51,160
Key takeaways.

76
00:06:51,160 --> 00:06:59,071
V I F above ten is serious multicollinearity; density scored fifteen point nine.

77
00:06:59,071 --> 00:07:07,278
For P C A, scale first; P C one plus P C two hold fifty percent of eleven features.

78
00:07:07,278 --> 00:07:11,234
Name each P C from its largest loadings.

79
00:07:11,234 --> 00:07:18,254
And L D A uses labels, while t-S N E and U M A P are for pictures only.

80
00:07:18,254 --> 00:07:20,232
Thanks for watching.

